                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <h3>
                            The guard stance is an essential part of kickboxing that helps you protect yourself from your opponent’s attacks. Here is a step-by-step guide to help you get started:
                        </h3>
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                   
                    <td>
                        <ul style="list-style-type:circle;margin:60px;">

                            <li>
                                Stand with your feet shoulder-width apart: This will give you a solid base and help you maintain your balance.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Raise your hands to your chin level: Your hands should be close to your face, with your elbows tucked in. This will help you protect your head and face from punches and kicks.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Keep your hands relaxed: You don’t want to tense up too much, as this can make it harder to move quickly
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Bend your knees slightly: This will help you stay light on your feet and be able to move quickly.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Keep your weight balanced: You don’t want to lean too far forward or backward, as this can make it harder to move quickly.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                 Keep your eyes on your opponent at all times and be ready to move quickly if they attack.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                      
                        <br />
                        <br />
                    </td>
